1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
When they need filler.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Yeah, so tons of bad stories.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
And again, is it because bad stories are normal?

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
How about just because bad stories have a better audience for them?

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Yes, please.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Wouldn't that just be a cultural part?

7
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Like, you'd be able to do some kind of Ferengi type society?

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Oh, the Ferengi?

9
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Yeah, so you would brag about the good deals you got and then not say the times you got scammed.

10
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Yeah, you know, there are subcultures of what bragging is not so friendly on.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
But, I mean, although, the general story is that bragging is most acceptable

12
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
when the people that are bragging can easily beat up people that don't like their bragging.

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
It's like bragging about athletic ability, but much easier.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Because people have a lot of athletic ability.

15
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,000
If you don't like it, they can intimidate you and beat you up.

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
But bragging about how you did really well on a test, for example, that can get you clobbered.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Yes?

18
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Oh, in this model, isn't complaining about the bad deals the reason why the model works?

19
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Because in complaining about that, this is why everyone knows that there's a risk behind you.

20
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Well, I mean, you don't need to have differential complaining.

21
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
So, you know, like, yes, so like, the model could work just as well if everyone,

22
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
after it happens, announces whether they got a good deal or bad deal.

23
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
I mean, in a way, actually, if people were naive and all that they ever heard were bad news,

24
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
this would lead to the market being destroyed even more often than the model says.

25
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Because, you know, so basically, if all you ever heard were negative feedback about a market,

26
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
the naive inference is that that's all that ever happens.

27
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
So you actually need to have in the background some kind of common sense

28
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
where people realize that the complainers are not representative.

29
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,000
All right.

30
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Okay, so your bottom line is in this model, on average,

31
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
buyers do benefit from whatever deals they actually make.

32
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
The problem in the model is just the market, is that the average selection shrinks the market,

33
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
makes it smaller than it otherwise could be, and in the extreme case, it just wipes the market out.

34
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
But it's not that the exchanges that happen are bad.

35
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
It's that it's unfortunate that more exchanges did not occur.

36
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
So, again, this is also not a model about why the sick are being ripped off in health insurance markets.

37
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,000
In fact, it's a model about how people that buy private health insurance are on average benefiting.

38
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
The average selection complaint is rather about who or what?

39
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,000
People who don't get insurance.

40
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Yes, it is about all the people that don't get insurance, even though they value more of the marginal cost,

41
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
the true marginal cost.

42
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
So, and again, in this model, people often look at this model and say, ah,

43
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
so this is the economic model proving that sick people will be able to buy insurance in the free market.

44
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
The model says exactly the opposite.

45
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
In this model, who are the only people that will buy insurance in the free market?

46
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Sick people.

47
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Who are the people that don't buy insurance?

48
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Healthy people.

49
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Right?

50
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
And you're like, huh, but that isn't what people are complaining about.

51
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Maybe their complaint is different from what the model is.

52
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
And well, then why do people equate the model with the popular complaint?

53
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Possibly intellectual laziness, where they're looking for, where they have,

54
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
the thing that's really bothering them, which is probably just the sick people have to pay more for health insurance.

55
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
And then they try, and then they latch on to a model that seems kind of related, even though it really isn't.

56
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Okay.

57
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
All right, now, adverse selection is economists' favorite argument for health insurance or for insurance regulation in general.

58
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:34,000
And in particular, the main regulation that this argues for is mandatory insurance.

59
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
And again, going back to this case.

60
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Suppose you ordered everyone to sell their companies regardless of their own desires.

61
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
You say every company must be sold.

62
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
You must divest.

63
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,000
All right?

64
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
So in that case, this gets rid of the adverse selection problem, thus by forcing everyone to sell.

65
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,000
All right?

66
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
So in that case, they're all going to be sold, and then buyers can know that the average value sold,

67
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:03,000
the company sold will be 50, and they'll be happy to pay, say, 51, and then all the mutual beneficial deals happen.

68
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:10,000
And similarly, if we're talking about health insurance, the idea here is that you require everyone to buy,

69
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,000
and this is going to be good or bad for healthy people when they're forced to buy?

70
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Bad.

71
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
But so then what is the argument for the regulation?

72
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
The argument is that it is a caloric improvement because the gains to the sick people exceed the losses to the healthy people.

73
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
And again, if you – from this example, it's clear that can be true.

74
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:41,000
So basically the idea is that if you force the healthy people in the pool, they're going to be worse off.

75
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Specifically, the healthiest people will definitely be worse off,

76
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:56,000
but the loss to them is going to be smaller than the gain to the others, and so it's a net social gain, and that is the real story.

77
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
All right, and we are now out of time, so we will continue with this next week.


